              "• <strong>Relaxation of Legal Standing:</strong> Any public-spirited citizen, lawyer, journalist, or social organization acting in good faith (<em>bona fide</em>) can approach the High Court or Supreme Court on behalf of people who cannot defend their own rights.<br/>• <strong>Letter Petitions (Informal Access):</strong> The court does not insist on formal legal petitions. Even an ordinary postcard, telegram, or letter sent by a citizen or prisoner can be treated directly as a writ petition under Article 32 (*Sunil Batra*).<br/>• <strong>Non-Adversarial Nature:</strong> PIL is not a battle between two private enemies. It is a collaborative effort between the citizen, the court, and the government to protect constitutional rights and uphold the rule of law.<br/>• <strong>Court Fact-Finding Commissions:</strong> Because poor victims lack resources to collect evidence, courts appoint independent advocate commissioners, district judges, or social scientists to investigate the facts on the ground (*Bandhua Mukti Morcha*)."
