            "heading": "I. The Protective Shield of Article 20",
            "paragraphs": [
              "When a citizen is accused of committing a crime, they stand alone against the immense investigative power, resources, and machinery of the State. Without constitutional protections, an authoritarian government could easily frame political opponents, invent retrospective crimes, or extract confessions through brutal custodial torture.",
              "To prevent such tyranny, <strong>Article 20 of the Constitution</strong> establishes three absolute, non-derogable safeguards for accused persons:<br/>1. <strong>Protection against Ex-Post Facto Laws (Article 20(1))</strong>,<br/>2. <strong>Protection against Double Jeopardy (Article 20(2))</strong>, and<br/>3. <strong>Privilege against Self-Incrimination (Article 20(3))</strong>.<br/>Under the 44th Constitutional Amendment (1978), Article 20 is so sacred that it <strong>cannot be suspended even during a National Emergency</strong> under Article 359."
            ]
