            "heading": "II. Protection Against Ex-Post Facto Laws (Article 20(1))",
            "paragraphs": [
              "An <strong>ex-post facto law</strong> is a retrospective criminal law—a law that imposes penalties on acts that were completely lawful at the time they were done, or a law that increases the punishment for a past crime. Article 20(1) contains two distinct prohibitions:<br/>• <strong>1. No Retrospective Criminal Offence:</strong> No person shall be convicted of any offence except for violation of a law in force at the time of the commission of the act charged as an offence. An act that was innocent yesterday cannot be declared a crime today and punished retroactively.<br/>• <strong>2. No Greater Penalty:</strong> No person shall be subjected to a penalty greater than that which might have been inflicted under the law in force at the time of the commission of the offence.<br/>• <em>Vital Exception (Beneficial Retrospective Laws):</em> Article 20(1) prohibits retrospective enhancement of punishment. If an amendment <strong>reduces or mitigates</strong> the punishment, the accused is entitled to claim the benefit of the lesser penalty (*Ratan Lal v. State of Punjab*). Furthermore, Article 20(1) applies only to criminal trials, not to civil liabilities, taxation laws, or preventive detention."
            ]
